WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.860
Though hardcore fans may have seen the John
Wick movies countless times and combed through

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.720
them for references, fun facts, and more,
the mysterious world of everyone's favorite

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.780
dog-loving assassin still holds plenty of
secrets.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.780
Thanks to the incredibly detailed world of
the John Wick films, from John's intense backstory

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:22.339
as a hitman nicknamed "Baba Yaga" to the hotel
that caters to some of the world's deadliest

00:00:22.339 --> 00:00:26.749
assassins, many fans might have assumed that
the first film was based on a novel and adapted

00:00:26.749 --> 00:00:27.340
for film.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.640
Believe it or not, John Wick was actually
a completely original script.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.340
In 2012, writer Derek Kolstad developed the
idea and started work on a project originally

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:42.720
entitled Scorn, about a man who trafficked
in evil and finally gets some sort of redemption,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:43.720
only to lose it.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.720
"You know the consequences."

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.520
"I'm not that guy anymore."

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.320
After a few studios showed interest, he sold
it to Thunder Road Pictures because they promised

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.240
to make the movie as soon as possible.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.900
Though Wick was originally written as a much
older retired hitman, Kolstad revised the

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.720
script once Thunder Road purchased the rights,
aided by Keanu Reeves.

00:01:05.869 --> 00:01:10.119
The film might not have been based on a comic
book or video game, but the character of John

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.480
Wick has made the leap to both.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.160
Dynamite Entertainment has since released
a comic book series about Wick's origin story,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:21.260
and gamers can get into Wick's mindset by
playing a VR shooter called John Wick Chronicles.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.360
For the full effect, though, make sure you
play a few hours of Nintendogs first.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:30.980
From Bill &amp; Ted to Speed, Keanu Reeves has
appeared in an impressive roster of famous

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:31.480
films.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:36.580
In 1999, however, he stepped into what was
considered to be a career-defining role as

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:41.689
Neo in the Matrix film series, a trilogy and
universe that set new standards for action

00:01:41.689 --> 00:01:46.020
films and gave pop culture one of its best
theories about alternate realities.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:51.800
As the techno-mystical Neo, "The One" who
can control the synthetic reality of the Matrix,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:56.189
Reeves was cemented as a true action star,
which made him an ideal star for the story

00:01:56.189 --> 00:01:59.020
of the assassin that all the other assassins
are afraid of.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:05.220
"I once saw him kill three men in a bar…
with a pencil."

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.780
Beyond sharing a lead actor, The Matrix and
the John Wick film series have quite a lot

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:10.280
in common.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.980
The two directors on the first film, David
Leitch and Chad Stahelski, did stunts on The

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:18.120
Matrix, and Stahelski was actually Reeves'
stuntman.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.680
John Wick: Chapter 2 reunited Reeves with
Laurence Fishburne, who appeared as Neo's

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:27.520
mentor Morpheus in The Matrix, and die hard
fans may have caught another connection as

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.860
well; Randall Duk Kim, who played the Keymaker
in The Matrix Reloaded, showed up as a doctor

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.440
in the first John Wick film.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.860
Keanu Reeves had plenty of input on the film,
and worked closely with writer David Kolstad,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:43.750
who said Reeves was closely attuned to even
the smallest details that could help flesh

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.860
out his otherwise mysterious character.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.320
With that in mind, it's no surprise that he
turned to former crew members from The Matrix

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.800
to help him create a great action film, not
just for stunts, but for the directors.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.850
Though Reeves initially reached out to David
Leitch and Chad Stahelski to see if the two

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:03.500
veteran fight choreographers would simply
design the film's action, he hoped they would

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.000
direct.

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.560
Luckily for Reeves, they were more than happy
to take on the challenge.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.760
Having worked with Reeves extensively in the
past, Leitch and Stahelski were familiar with

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.280
both his prowess and his process, making stunts
and fights on John Wick that much more seamless.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.200
Both Leitch and Stahelski have credited their
work on The Matrix as an inspiration for John

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.540
Wick as well as a guiding light for their
overall careers.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.280
Stahelski has said that not just John Wick,
but most films he's worked on wouldn't exist

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.440
without The Matrix, and both directors have
said that watching Lana and Lilly Wachowski

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:38.700
work was a master class in directing action
that still had an emotional core.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:43.800
Throughout his career, Keanu Reeves has become
an experienced stuntman in his own right,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.100
learning countless forms of fighting and performing
many of his own stunts.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.760
John Wick is no exception.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:52.800
To prepare for the role, Reeves spent months
training with veterans from SWAT teams as

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.700
well as former Navy SEALs.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.300
This was largely due to Leitch and Stahelski's
insistence that the star learn entirely new

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:04.200
methods of fighting for the film rather than
relying on his previous martial arts experience.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.340
Reeves went on to do a large amount of the
stunts seen in John Wick, and most of them

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:12.990
with no double in John Wick Chapter 2, where
the stunts and fights far surpassed the first

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:16.520
film, upping the ante for Reeves as well as
for loyal viewers.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.320
Early footage from John Wick 3 shows even
more death-defying action, proving that even

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.760
as he ages, he's still more than capable of
stunning audiences with his stunt work.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:31.400
Actors sidelining as producers isn't uncommon,
but one of the producers responsible for John

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.280
Wick's arrival into movie theaters might still
come as a surprise to most fans.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.880
According to the DVD commentary, Eva Longoria,
best known for her role on Desperate Housewives

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:45.780
as well as her various modeling contracts,
is accredited producer on the first Wick film.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.570
Leitch and Stahelski both noted that they've
never met her, but they're grateful to her

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:50.640
for "writing a check."

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.360
"Denomination?"

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.120
"Seven million."

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:56.840
"Processing, please hold."

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.860
Whether you knew it or not, we have Longoria
to thank for funding one of the century's

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:01.940
best action movies.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:07.060
Leitch and Stahelski obviously drew from their
experience working on other action films,

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:11.760
but John Wick also drew its unique tone from
everything from classic action movies to horror

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:12.260
novels.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:17.000
The two directors cited several direct influences
on the film, which ranged from the classic

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.700
Clint Eastwood Western The Good, The Bad and
the Ugly to crime films like Point Blank and

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.080
foreign releases like The Killer, directed
by Hong Kong action auteur John Woo.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:30.800
Interestingly, while the film has the feeling
of a neo-noir thriller, the directors have

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:33.960
said that there wasn't a lot of noir influence
on John Wick.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.460
Instead, they invoked directors like Sergio
Leone and Akira Kurosawa instead.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:44.120
Kurosawa in particular, well known for incredibly
influential samurai films like Seven Samurai,

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:48.840
Yojimbo, and Throne of Blood comes up quite
frequently when the two discuss the first

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:49.840
film in the series.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:54.620
Beyond Kurosawa, Leitch and Stahelski have
also mentioned looking to classic action stars

00:05:54.620 --> 00:06:00.050
and directors from the 1970s and 1980s for
inspiration, but their world-building was

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.340
influenced by something outside of film.

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:07.680
Writer Derek Kolstad mentioned Stephen King
as a huge influence, especially to show how

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:12.300
far a broken man will go, a common theme in
King's many horror novels.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.280
Even though Stahelski and Leitch technically
co-directed the first John Wick film, the

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:22.160
rules of the Director's Guild of America meant
that only Stahelski could be credited as the

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.620
director, while Leitch was instead listed
among the film's producers.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:30.320
Though the two clearly maintained a good professional
relationship, they parted ways for the second

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:35.280
film, and Stahelski went on to direct John
Wick Chapter 2 as well as John Wick Chapter

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.860
3: Parabellum by himself.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.720
Meanwhile, Leitch embarked on an incredibly
lucrative career of his own.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:46.699
The same year Stahelski helmed John Wick 2,
Leitch directed Atomic Blonde, an action thriller

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:51.450
starring Charlize Theron that also immediately
became famous for its incredible action set

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:55.180
pieces, especially its signature one-take
fight in a stairwell.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.540
After that, Leitch took the reins on Deadpool
2, the second outing for Ryan Reynolds' foul-mouthed

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:04.340
mercenary, and Fast &amp; Furious Presents: Hobbs
and Shaw.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:09.180
Clearly, his departure from John Wick didn't
hurt this stuntman-turned-director one bit,

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.640
and he also stays in touch with Stahelski
to help with ideas in an advisory role.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.080
It's fortunate for the John Wick series that
it's been able to retain most of the creative

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.460
team throughout all of the movies, especially
director Chad Stahelski and writer Derek Kolstad,

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.320
both of whom have been working on Wick since
the very beginning.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:31.440
It's clear that there's plenty of material
to mine from the John Wick universe, and Stahelski,

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.740
along with plenty of audience members, hopes
that the series goes on for quite some time.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:40.420
Stahelski noted that Reeves was heavily responsible
for the story behind his character, and said

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:44.520
that he would happily spend the rest of his
career existing within John Wick's dangerous,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:46.139
violent world, adding,

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.259
"I enjoy making these movies because there's
no limit.

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:50.920
We create our own mythology."

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:55.840
Considering the success Stahelski has enjoyed
with the Wick franchise thus far, it's certainly

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.680
understandable that he'd be content to keep
telling this seamlessly endless story.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:04.800
One of the many things that fans love about
the John Wick series is the careful and detailed

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:09.620
world in which the main character lives and
kills, which is full of small details without

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.300
being obvious or clichéd.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.740
Throughout most of the first film, audiences
are actually unaware of Wick's past until

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:20.620
other criminals realize that one of the most
dangerous hitmen alive is coming out of "retirement."

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:27.660
"People keep asking if I'm back, and I haven't
really had an answer… but now yeah, I'm

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.360
thinking I'm back."

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:33.880
It isn't until he returns to the Continental,
the heart of this underground world, to begin

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:37.640
seeking his revenge, that the world-building
truly begins in earnest.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:42.720
In interviews, Leitch and Stahelski have divulged
plenty of details about the world of the film,

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:46.020
including little ones that fans may not have
caught during a casual viewing.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.540
One such detail is that no "innocents" die
in the John Wick series, which includes police

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:56.310
officers, and not only are police officers
spared by the assassins, they have their own

00:08:56.310 --> 00:09:00.810
underground which works in tandem with the
criminal underground, and each side essentially

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:02.820
leaves the other alone to do their job.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:04.560
"You, uh, working again?"

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:06.600
"No, just sorting some stuff out."

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:11.600
All in all, it's pretty sophisticated for
a worldwide crime syndicate responsible for

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:12.800
brutal murders.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:17.700
Yet another perfect example of world-building
in the John Wick series is the use of the

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.930
coins, which hitmen like Wick can use as currency
for everything from a hotel room to a well-stirred

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:25.820
cocktail or discrete dead body disposal.

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:31.100
Since these prices seem fairly inconsistent,
to say the least, fans have worked tirelessly

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:35.440
to try and figure out exactly how the gold
coin-based economy actually works within the

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:36.460
criminal underground.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:41.120
"Hey Harry… you keen on earning a coin?"

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:46.120
Some experts have even weighed in to say that
this is fairly realistic when it comes to

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:51.120
organized crime, while others have speculated
that by pricing everything at the same point,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:55.750
the Continental prevents assassins from overstaying
their welcome at the hotel, forcing them to

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:58.400
use it as a simple stop during their missions.

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:03.100
As far as the directors are concerned, their
view is that while the pricing is all over

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:07.520
the place, it also doesn't matter, because
that's not really what the coins are about.

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:11.680
Leitch and Stahelski have said that they're
essentially "business cards" that allow point

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.540
of entry as well as indicating that the coin-holder
is "in the know."

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:19.840
Wondering about the currency value is, ultimately,
missing the point.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.780
It's about the custom, not about getting change
back for your closet full of AK-47s.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:28.940
The hotel in the films, the Continental, is
shrouded in mystery.

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:33.440
There are a few very important, very clear
rules, including the use of the gold coins

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:36.260
and the absolute prohibition of killing on
the grounds…

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.020
"And the Continental?"

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:44.240
"You killed a man on company grounds, Jonathan,
you leave me no choice but to declare you

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:45.560
excommunicado."

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.040
.... but naturally, fans want to know a whole
lot more about how this place works.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:55.300
Luckily, the minds behind John Wick, including
star Keanu Reeves, are working on a new part

00:10:55.300 --> 00:11:00.240
of their world that might explain some long-standing
mysteries about the hotel, its guests, and

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:00.740
its history.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:06.040
In early 2018, it was announced that Starz
would be developing a television series based

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:11.590
on the John Wick universe called The Continental
which would, as the name implies, focus on

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:12.420
the hotel.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:16.960
Reeves is listed as an executive producer
on the project, and executives from the network

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:20.640
told fans they shouldn't rule out seeing him
appear on the show as well.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:25.160
Writer Derek Kolstad will also be working
on the show alongside Leitch and Stahelski,

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:29.340
lending added credibility to a project we're
already excited about.

